<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=geothermal-power">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geothermal power</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as geothermal electricity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geothermal power is electrical power generated from geothermal energy, using dry steam, flash steam or binary cycle power stations. Prince Piero Ginori Conti tested the first geothermal power generator on 4 July 1904 at Larderello, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where it lit four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb">light bulbs</a>, and the first commercial station was built there in 1911. Italy was the only industrial producer until 1958, when Wairakei in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> was commissioned. The Geysers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> began operating in 1960, and a binary cycle station was first demonstrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geothermal-power.jpg" alt="Geothermal power" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gretar Ívarsson – Edited by Fir0002 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NesjavellirPowerPlant_edit2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Turkey is the 15th largest electricity producer, about half of it renewable, and the fourth in <b>geothermal power</b>; its first nuclear station is Akkuyu and the Sakarya gas field began production in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
